{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Berthold1969-47L-wideband-ZnS-ThF4",
  "Source": "J. Berthold, \"Computer Design and Fabrication Techniques for a Wideband Dielectric Mirror,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 1919-1924 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.001919 - Table I p. 1921 (layer QWOTs in um and indices); design discussion p. 1921-1922; Fig. 4",
  "Notes": "Wideband dielectric mirror with ~1-2 % transmission for an optical parametric oscillator (three interleaved stacks: 0.41/0.49 um, 0.55/0.70 um, 0.78/0.89 um, then iterated)\nSpecification: Bandwidth 6000 cm^-1 centred at 14400 cm^-1 (11400-17400 cm^-1, i.e. 574.7-877.2 nm) with approximately constant 1-2 % transmission; the computer calculation covered 10000-18000 cm^-1 every 50 cm^-1, nonabsorbing layers, normal incidence.\nReported: p. 1922 and abstract: theoretical bandwidth 6000 cm^-1 centred about 14400 cm^-1 (694.4 nm); transmission varies between 0.6 % and 2.6 % across the band, with one point of 7.4 % transmission (a peak placed deliberately for the OPO) at the 14400 cm^-1 centre. Fig. 4 (calculated vs experimental R, 10000-18000 cm^-1): calculated R has a sharp dip to ~92.5 % near 14400 cm^-1 and maxima ~99-99.5 % elsewhere in the band (read off the plot, approximate); experimental agrees within 1 % except at the band ends (ZnS dispersion); the experimental curve was shifted to correct a 370 A shift to shorter wavelength caused by a substrate-position error. Sum of the 47 QWOTs (computed here, not printed) = 29.01 um.\nNotes: Table I numbers layers from the substrate (layer 1 = ThF4 adhesion layer on the quartz substrate, 'the first dielectric layer placed on the substrate'); the list here is reversed so layer 1 = Table I layer 47 (n = 1.51, next to air). Table I gives the quarter-wave optical thickness QWOT = 4 n l in um (Eq. 1), i.e. the wavelength at which each layer is a quarter wave; with reference_nm = 1000 the QWOT count equals the printed um value (e.g. 0.63 um -> 0.63 QWOT at 1000 nm; physical thickness l = QWOT/(4n), e.g. Table I layer 1 = 630/(4*1.48) = 106.4 nm). Indices: 2.40 = ZnS, 1.51 = ThF4 (Table I footnote); Table I layer 1 is ThF4 with n = 1.48 (lowered by stress on quartz, p. 1923), so it gets its own material. All layers nonabsorbing and dispersionless as in the paper's calculation. The substrate is quartz with no index given; database SiO2 used (the paper neglected substrate absorption; whether the back surface was included is not stated - assume a semi-infinite substrate). Air incident, coated side facing the light, normal incidence. Layer count 47 matches the title and Table I (24 low-index incl. layer 1, 23 ZnS). The merit TA target 0.015 is an interpretation of 'approximately 1-2 % transmission'; the actual design was hand-iterated. During construction the ZnS index for the first ZnS layer on each new monitor slide was set to 2.35 in the monitoring program; this affects monitoring, not the Table I design.\nLayers from Table I p. 1921 (layer QWOTs in um and indices); design discussion p. 1921-1922; Fig. 4; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours T 7.32 % at 694.4 nm; elsewhere in the band 0.454 % to 2.61 %; paper T 0.6-2.6 % across 575-877 nm, 7.4 % at the centre.",
